              %li
                %a{:href => "#in_topos_theory"} In topos theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#summary Summary
          %p There are really two notions of one-sided real number: lower reals and upper reals. But there is a nearly perfect symmetry between them (although the lower reals seem to get slightly more use).
          %p
            These are largely concepts in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            as the values taken by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+measures"} positive measures
            (and more generally are used to define
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+positive+cones"} extended positive cones
          %p
            However, even in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            as a lower real and include
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
            as an upper real. (However, you can restrict to
            %em bounded
            lower/upper reals to avoid that, or alternatively genralise to
            %em extended
            lower/upper reals if you want to include both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ±
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm\infty
            at once.) Also, the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+structure"} topology
            on the lower reals is the
            %em
              lower
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicontinuous+topology"} semicontinuous topology
            (and the natural topology on the upper reals is the
            = succeed ")." do
              %em upper semicontinuous topology
          %p
            One-sided numbers are sometimes called (upper or lower)
            %em semicontinuous
            numbers. To see why, consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicontinuous+functions"} semicontinuous functions
            (real-valued) on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            to (classically) the set of real numbers equipped with the semicontinuous topology or to (constructively) a locale whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+locale"} points
            are the one-sided real numbers. On the other hand, these semicontinuous functions are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal
            one-sided real numbers in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+of+sheaves"} topos of sheaves
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %p
            Lower and upper reals don't interact well together. Their “intersection” is the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+numbers"} MacNeille real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/located+real+numbers"} located real numbers
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            (In the following, ‘number’ without qualification may be taken to mean either a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            or a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subset"} dense set
            of real numbers.)
          %p
            A
            %strong lower real number
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            set of numbers. A
            %strong bounded lower real
            is the supremum of an inhabited set of numbers that has a finite upper bound. An
            %strong extended lower real
            is the supremum of an arbitrary set of numbers.
          %p
            An
            %strong upper real number
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infimum"} infimum
            of an inhabited set of numbers. A
            %strong bounded upper real
            is the infimum of an inhabited set of numbers that has a finite lower bound. An
            %strong extended upper real
            is the infimum of an arbitrary set of numbers.
          %p
            We cannot generalize further by taking more extrema of the same sort. Explicitly, the supremum of any inhabited set of lower reals is a lower real and the infimum of any inhabited set of upper reals is an upper real. Similar results obtain if we use either bounded or extended reals, so long as we also either require the set to bounded or allow it to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+subset"} empty
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            There are some choices as to how exactly to represent one-sided real numbers; we will define them here as certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            with any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subset"} dense subset
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          %h3#lower_reals Lower reals
          %p
            An
            %strong extended lower real number
            is any subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            such that
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              for some
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in L
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in L
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
